  "id": "Compound::DB00819 - Disease::DOID:1686",
  "verbalization": "Acetazolamide, a sulfonamide derivative, is implicated in the pathogenesis of glaucoma through multiple mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that Acetazolamide may contribute to glaucoma development via its role in causing hepatic insufficiency, which is subsequently treated with drugs like Diclofenamide and Methazolamide. These latter drugs are carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, a class of compounds that are also used in the treatment of glaucoma. The shared ancestry with Acetazolamide through sulfonamide and carbonic anhydrase inhibitor pathways underscores a potential mechanistic link between Acetazolamide and glaucoma, particularly through the inhibition of carbonic anhydrase, which is involved in the regulation of aqueous humor production. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Acetazolamide in the pathophysiology of glaucoma, although the direct therapeutic effect of Acetazolamide on glaucoma remains to be fully elucidated.",
